"[2610.14849] StateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a Reusable Sparse GKR Engine (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14849)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14849\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 166\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Cryptography and Security\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 10 Oct 2026 (【5†v1】), last revised 26 Oct 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:StateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a Reusable Sparse GKR Engine\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Jinwook Kim】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled StateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a Reusable Sparse GKR Engine, by Jinwook Kim\nL14: \nL15: 【7†View PDF】 【8†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Authenticated state transitions need proof engines that conserve memory, avoid repeated setup, keep every result bound to its request under parallel execution and return complete proofs at service rates. We present StateSync-GKR, a reusable Rust sumcheck/GKR core with a sparse-Merkle frontend, and evaluate release 1.2.0 against release 1.1 measured on the same day on the same instance types, request corpus and latency budgets.\nL17: Sparse layer reduction keeps additional requested allocation 3,242-fold and peak process memory about 89-fold below the engine's dense reference at width 4,096.\nL18: Release 1.2 changes two parts of the software while proof bytes and verifier acceptance stay fixed: reorganized round arithmetic raises prepared proving throughput 2.4 to 2.7-fold on a 192-core bare-metal host, and a new serving path starts each job as soon as a worker is free, bounds queued work and buffered replies, and returns proofs in completion order bound to request identifiers. On a 192-core VM at 1,000 requests/s, 99th-percentile latency to verified acceptance falls from 440 to 34 ms.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of aReusable Sparse GKR Engine (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.14849v1)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.14849v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 344\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: 【0†Back to arXiv】\nL13: \nL14: 【1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org】 【2†Report Issue】 【3†Back to Abstract】 【4†Download PDF】\nL15:   1. 【5†Abstract】\nL16:   2. 【6†1 Introduction】\nL17:   3. 【7†2 Engine and application boundaries】\nL18:     1. 【8†2.1 Layer reduction and structured wiring】\nL19:     2. 【9†2.2 Cube gates and path constraints】\nL20:     3. 【10†2.3 Preparation and independent requests】\nL21:     4. 【11†2.4 Semantics, transport and reuse】\nL22:     5. 【12†2.5 Formal results as a design basis】\nL23:     6. 【13†2.6 What the application requests mean】\nL24:   4. 【14†3 Experimental design】\nL25:     1. 【15†3.1 Fixed source and separate cohorts】\nL26:     2. 【16†3.2 External comparison: what was and was not established】\nL27:     3. 【17†3.3 Same-driver memory contrast】\nL28:     4. 【18†3.4 Arrival and acceptance endpoint】\nL29:       1. 【19†Why offered rates are controlled.】\nL30:     5. 【20†3.5 Statistics and clocks】\nL31:       1. 【21†Reading the main contrasts.】\nL32:   5. 【22†4 Memory, scale and result preservation】\nL33:   6. 【23†5 Completed computation and reusable structure】\nL34:   7. 【24†6 Serving profiles and configuration choices】\nL35:     1. 【25†6.1 A confirmed 48-core baseline】\nL36:     2. 【26†6.2 Equal-total-resource deployment】\nL37:     3. 【27†6.3 Fine transit