edge framework that certifies auditor-defined properties of a trained checkpoint through forward evaluation rather than verification of its optimization trajectory.\nL17: zkLLMPoT includes 2 phases: 1) The trainer fixes the architecture and the model weights are committed. Then the auditor selects challenge sequences, preventing the trainer from modifying the checkpoint in response to the audit data. 2) Then the trainer proves the objective value attained by the committed model on those sequences. This formulation makes the certification cost independent of the number of training iterations, without revealing model weights or requiring access to private training data.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nspec_extend.rs - source (https://doc.rust-lang.org/src/alloc/vec/spec_extend.rs.html)\nciteturn2view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://doc.rust-lang.org/src/alloc/vec/spec_extend.rs.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 67\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: ## Files\nL3: #\nL4: \nL5: alloc/vec/\nL6: spec_extend.rs\nL7: \nL8:     `【1†1】use core::clone::TrivialClone;\nL9:     【2†2】use core::iter::TrustedLen;\nL10:     【3†3】use core::slice;\nL11:     【4†4】\nL12:     【5†5】use super::{IntoIter, Vec};\nL13:     【6†6】use crate::alloc::Allocator;\nL14:     【7†7】\nL15:     【8†8】// Specialization trait used for Vec::extend\nL16:     【9†9】pub(super) trait SpecExtend<T, I> {\nL17:     【10†10】    fn spec_extend(&mut self, iter: I);\nL18:     【11†11】}\nL19:     【12†12】\nL20:     【13†13】impl<T, I, A: Allocator> SpecExtend<T, I> for Vec<T, A>\nL21:     【14†14】where\nL22:     【15†15】    I: Iterator<Item = T>,\nL23:     【16†16】{\nL24:     【17†17】    default fn spec_extend(&mut self, iter: I) {\nL25:     【18†18】        self.extend_desugared(iter)\nL26:     【19†19】    }\nL27:     【20†20】}\nL28:     【21†21】\nL29:     【22†22】impl<T, I, A: Allocator> SpecExtend<T, I> for V